# Detailed Extraction of Microsoft PowerPoint Elements via COM

This document provides a comprehensive, low-level guide to extracting information from Microsoft PowerPoint presentations using COM automation. It emphasizes shapes, charts, groupings, and canvases, with a detailed focus on converting native PowerPoint charts to SVG, including embedded text and data. The guide is generic, suitable for any COM-compatible programming environment (e.g., TypeScript with `winax`), and includes enumerated values, detailed pseudocode, and practical considerations for low-level extraction and SVG conversion.

## 1. COM Automation and the PowerPoint Object Model

COM enables programmatic control of Microsoft PowerPoint through its object model, exposing objects like `Application`, `Presentation`, `Slide`, `Shapes`, `GroupShapes`, and `Chart`. This guide focuses on low-level access to these objects to extract detailed properties and map them to SVG.

### 1.1. Key Objects and Their Roles
- **Application**: The PowerPoint application instance (`PowerPoint.Application`).
- **Presentation**: An open PowerPoint file (`Presentations.Open`).
- **Slide**: A single slide in the presentation (`Slides` collection).
- **Shapes**: Shapes on a slide (e.g., rectangles, images, charts) in the `Shapes` collection.
- **GroupShapes**: Shapes within a group (`GroupItems` collection).
- **Chart**: Chart objects (`msoChart`) with data and formatting.
- **TextFrame**: Text container within shapes or charts.
- **Canvas**: PowerPoint uses "drawing canvases" less explicitly than Word, but grouped shapes or placeholders can serve similar roles.

### 1.2. Enumerated Values
Below are key enumerated values from the PowerPoint Object Model (based on `Mso` and `Pp` constants). These are critical for identifying and processing elements.

#### Shape Types (`MsoShapeType`)
- `msoAutoShape` (1): Geometric shapes (e.g., rectangles, circles).
- `msoCallout` (2): Callout shapes.
- `msoChart` (3): Chart objects.
- `msoFreeform` (7): Custom-drawn shapes.
- `msoGroup` (6): Grouped shapes.
- `msoEmbeddedOLEObject` (8): Embedded objects (e.g., Excel tables).
- `msoPicture` (13): Images.
- `msoPlaceholder` (14): Placeholders for text, images, or charts.
- `msoTextBox` (17): Text boxes.
- `msoMedia` (16): Audio or video.

#### AutoShape Types (`MsoAutoShapeType`)
- `msoShapeRectangle` (1): Rectangle.
- `msoShapeOval` (9): Circle or ellipse.
- `msoShapeLine` (20): Straight line.
- `msoShapeArc` (25): Arc.
- `msoShapeFreeform` (5): Custom polygon.
- (Full list: ~150 types; see [MsoAutoShapeType](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/office/vba/api/office.msoautoshapetype)).

#### Fill Types (`MsoFillType`)
- `msoFillSolid` (0): Solid color.
- `msoFillPatterned` (1): Pattern (e.g., stripes).
- `msoFillGradient` (2): Gradient.
- `msoFillTextured` (3): Texture.
- `msoFillPicture` (5): Image fill.

#### Line Styles (`MsoLineStyle`)
- `msoLineSingle` (1): Solid line.
- `msoLineSimple` (2): Dashed line.
- `msoLineThickThin` (4): Thick-thin double line.
- (See [MsoLineStyle](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/office/vba/api/office.msolinestyle)).

#### Chart Types (`XlChartType`)
- `xlColumnClustered` (51): Clustered column.
- `xlColumnStacked` (52): Stacked column.
- `xlLine` (4): Line chart.
- `xlPie` (5): Pie chart.
- `xlArea` (1): Area chart.
- (See [XlChartType](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/office/vba/api/excel.xlcharttype)).

#### Placeholder Types (`PpPlaceholderType`)
- `ppPlaceholderTitle` (1): Slide title.
- `ppPlaceholderBody` (2): Body text.
- `ppPlaceholderChart` (8): Chart placeholder.
- `ppPlaceholderPicture` (10): Image placeholder.
- (See [PpPlaceholderType](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/office/vba/api/powerpoint.ppplaceholdertype)).

### 1.3. Prerequisites
- Microsoft PowerPoint installed.
- COM-compatible environment (e.g., TypeScript with `winax`).
- Familiarity with PowerPoint Object Model (see [Microsoft PowerPoint VBA Reference](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/office/vba/api/overview/powerpoint)).
- SVG knowledge for conversion (see [SVG 2 Specification](https://www.w3.org/TR/SVG2/)).

## 2. Low-Level Extraction Process

This section details the steps to extract shapes, charts, groupings, and canvases from PowerPoint slides, including all relevant properties and enumerated values.

### 2.1. Opening and Accessing a Presentation
**Steps**:
1. Create a `PowerPoint.Application` instance.
2. Set `Visible = false` for background processing.
3. Open the presentation using `Presentations.Open(FileName, ReadOnly: true)`.
4. Iterate through `Slides` to access each slide’s `Shapes` collection.
5. Close the presentation and quit the application.

**Pseudocode**:
```pseudocode
app = CreateObject("PowerPoint.Application")
app.Visible = false
pres = app.Presentations.Open("path/to/presentation.pptx", ReadOnly: true)
for each slide in pres.Slides
    // Process slide shapes (see below)
pres.Close()
app.Quit()
```

**Properties to Extract**:
- `Presentation.Name`: Presentation filename.
- `Presentation.Path`: File path.
- `Slide.Width`, `Slide.Height`: Slide dimensions in points.
- `Slide.SlideNumber`: Slide number.

### 2.2. Extracting Shapes
Shapes are objects on a slide, stored in the `Shapes` collection. Unlike Word, PowerPoint shapes include placeholders and slide-specific elements.

**Steps**:
1. Iterate through `slide.Shapes` for each slide.
2. Check `Type` to identify the shape (e.g., `msoAutoShape`, `msoChart`, `msoPlaceholder`).
3. Extract geometric properties (`Left`, `Top`, `Width`, `Height`, `Rotation`).
4. Extract appearance (`Fill`, `Line`).
5. Extract text via `TextFrame.TextRange` if `HasTextFrame = true`.
6. Handle special cases (e.g., placeholders, images, freeforms).

**Key Properties**:
- `Type`: See `MsoShapeType` above.
- `AutoShapeType`: For `msoAutoShape` shapes (e.g., `msoShapeRectangle`).
- `PlaceholderFormat.Type`: For `msoPlaceholder` (e.g., `ppPlaceholderChart`).
- `Left`, `Top`: Position in points (relative to slide top-left).
- `Width`, `Height`: Size in points.
- `Rotation`: Angle in degrees.
- `Fill.Type`: See `MsoFillType` (e.g., `msoFillSolid`).
- `Fill.ForeColor.RGB`: Fill color (RGB value).
- `Line.Style`: See `MsoLineStyle`.
- `Line.Weight`: Line thickness in points.
- `TextFrame.TextRange.Text`: Text content.
- `TextFrame.TextRange.Font`: Font properties (`Name`, `Size`, `Color`).
- `ZOrderPosition`: Stacking order.

**Pseudocode**:
```pseudocode
for each slide in pres.Slides
    print "Slide:", slide.SlideNumber
    for each shape in slide.Shapes
        print "Type:", shape.Type
        if shape.Type = msoAutoShape then
            print "AutoShapeType:", shape.AutoShapeType
        if shape.Type = msoPlaceholder then
            print "PlaceholderType:", shape.PlaceholderFormat.Type
        print "Position:", shape.Left, shape.Top
        print "Size:", shape.Width, shape.Height
        print "Rotation:", shape.Rotation
        print "Fill:", shape.Fill.Type, shape.Fill.ForeColor.RGB
        print "Line:", shape.Line.Style, shape.Line.Weight
        if shape.HasTextFrame and shape.TextFrame.HasText then
            print "Text:", shape.TextFrame.TextRange.Text
            print "Font:", shape.TextFrame.TextRange.Font.Name, shape.TextFrame.TextRange.Font.Size
```

### 2.3. Extracting Grouped Shapes
Grouped shapes (`msoGroup`) contain multiple shapes in `GroupItems`.

**Steps**:
1. Identify groups (`Type = msoGroup`).
2. Iterate through `shape.GroupItems`.
3. Extract properties recursively for each sub-shape.

**Key Properties**:
- `GroupItems.Count`: Number of shapes.
- `GroupItems.Item(Index)`: Access individual shapes.
- Same properties as regular shapes for sub-shapes.

**Pseudocode**:
```pseudocode
for each slide in pres.Slides
    for each shape in slide.Shapes
        if shape.Type = msoGroup then
            print "Group with", shape.GroupItems.Count, "items"
            for each subShape in shape.GroupItems
                print "SubShape Type:", subShape.Type
                print "SubShape Position:", subShape.Left, subShape.Top
                if subShape.HasTextFrame and subShape.TextFrame.HasText then
                    print "SubShape Text:", subShape.TextFrame.TextRange.Text
```

### 2.4. Extracting Canvases
PowerPoint does not use explicit "drawing canvases" like Word, but grouped shapes or placeholders can act as containers. Treat `msoGroup` or `msoPlaceholder` with multiple elements as canvas-like structures.

**Steps**:
1. Identify groups or placeholders containing multiple shapes.
2. Iterate through `GroupItems` or placeholder content.
3. Extract properties for each contained shape.

**Pseudocode**:
```pseudocode
for each slide in pres.Slides
    for each shape in slide.Shapes
        if shape.Type = msoGroup then
            print "Canvas-like Group with", shape.GroupItems.Count, "items"
            for each groupShape in shape.GroupItems
                print "GroupShape Type:", groupShape.Type
                print "GroupShape Position:", groupShape.Left, groupShape.Top
                if groupShape.HasTextFrame and groupShape.TextFrame.HasText then
                    print "GroupShape Text:", groupShape.TextFrame.TextRange.Text
```

### 2.5. Extracting Charts (Low-Level)
Charts (`msoChart`) are shapes containing data in an embedded Excel workbook and visual elements defined by properties. They can appear as `msoChart` or within `msoPlaceholder` (type `ppPlaceholderChart`).

**Steps**:
1. Identify charts (`Type = msoChart` or `PlaceholderFormat.Type = ppPlaceholderChart`).
2. Access `Chart` object (`shape.Chart`).
3. Extract chart type (`Chart.ChartType`).
4. Extract data via `Chart.ChartData.Workbook`.
5. Extract visual properties (e.g., title, legend, axes).
6. Extract series data and formatting.

**Key Properties**:
- `Chart.ChartType`: See `XlChartType` (e.g., `xlColumnClustered`).
- `Chart.Title.Text`: Chart title.
- `Chart.Legend`: Legend properties (`Position`, `Font`).
- `Chart.Axes`: Axis properties (`Title`, `Scale`).
- `Chart.SeriesCollection`: Data series (`Name`, `Values`, `XValues`).
- `Chart.ChartData.Workbook`: Embedded Excel workbook.
- `Chart.ChartData.Workbook.Worksheets(1).Range`: Data range.

**Data Extraction**:
- Activate the workbook: `Chart.ChartData.Activate`.
- Access the worksheet: `Chart.ChartData.Workbook.Worksheets(1)`.
- Read data ranges (e.g., `Range("A1:B10").Value`).

**Pseudocode**:
```pseudocode
for each slide in pres.Slides
